    "faqs": [
      {
        "q": "Is there a specific Indian law called the 'Personality Rights Act'?",
        "a": "No. India has no dedicated personality rights statute — protection is built through court rulings interpreting privacy, copyright, trademark, and IT law together."
      },
      {
        "q": "Can non-celebrities claim personality rights protection in India?",
        "a": "In principle yes, though courts generally expect a claimant to show identifiability and some commercial value or goodwill in their persona, which is often easier to demonstrate for well-known public figures."
      },
      {
        "q": "What is a 'John Doe' or 'Ashok Kumar' order?",
        "a": "It's a court order that grants relief against unnamed, unidentified defendants — commonly used in personality rights cases to block anonymous infringers before they can even be identified individually."
      },
      {
        "q": "Do personality rights cover satire, parody, or news coverage of a public figure?",
        "a": "Courts have generally treated genuine news reporting, commentary, and satire differently from commercial exploitation, though the exact boundary is still being worked out case by case."
      }
    ]
